#set document(
  title: "Lists — bullet and numbered markers with hanging indent",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
)

= Lists

Mosaic recognises two list flavours at the block level. An unordered
list is opened by a line starting with `- `; the marker renders as a
`•` in the gutter to the left of the item's text, and the layout
engine line-breaks long items while keeping every wrapped line flush
with the first line's left edge:

- A short item.
- A long item authored as a single source line — the greedy line breaker wraps it onto a second visual line where the wrapped text hangs flush with the first line's left edge, never sliding back under the marker.
- *Italic*, **bold**, and `inline code` flow through unchanged inside an item.

Source-line continuation (a second `.mos` line that's *not* a fresh marker line) is out of scope in this slice. Such a line opens a new paragraph block at the page margin rather than continuing the current item. Author long items on a single source line and let the layout engine break them.

== Nesting

A marker line indented deeper than its parent becomes a child of the
most recent item at the outer indent — the parser compares relative
indents, so any greater indentation works (two spaces is just the
house convention). Indents nest arbitrarily deep, and marker kinds
can change at deeper levels:

- Outer item one.
  - First nested child.
  - Second nested child, with *emphasis*.
- Outer item two.
  - Mixing marker kinds at a deeper level is fine:
    1. ordered grandchild,
    2. and another.
- Outer item three.

Marker kinds do **not** mix at the *same* indent — switching from
`- ` to `1. ` (or vice versa) at one level closes the first list and
opens a second adjacent one:

- unordered item, ends this list,
1. ordered item, starts a new one.

== Numbered lists renumber from 1

The digits typed in the source are ignored at render time — the layout
engine numbers each item starting from 1, in source order:

5. first step,
9. second step,
27. and a long third step authored as a single source line that runs well past the right margin so the layout engine has to wrap it onto a second visual line at the same indent as the first — proving the hanging indent survives across wraps in numbered lists too.

== Right-aligned markers

Markers right-align against the inside edge of the gutter so the dots
line up vertically across items of differing widths. Single-digit
items sit further right than `10.` or `12.`, which is the standard
typographic convention for numbered lists:

1. one,
2. two,
3. three,
4. four,
5. five,
6. six,
7. seven,
8. eight,
9. nine,
10. ten,
11. eleven,
12. twelve.

The gutter floor (`LIST_MARKER_GUTTER_PT`) accommodates up to
two-digit markers at the default body size. Three-digit ordered lists
trigger the adaptive gutter — the layout engine shapes every marker
the list will emit and widens the gutter to fit the worst case — so a
200-item list still keeps its `200.` clear of the indented text
column.
